raduate academic  Awards is 1979 Rhodes Scholar to that  Jeffery Jackson the Academy provides an  excellent background in education and  experience for a wide variety of careers  in today's Air Force the Air Force is a  very technical oriented service and they  need engineers and scientists to  continue their mission and the major  emphasis here at the Academy is on  engineering courses and everyone is  required even though political science  majors are required to take aeronautical  engineering and dynamics something to  give them a basic understanding of  Science and Engineering in the same  light the electrical engineer is  required to take human values course in  English literature and history courses  on the American Way of war  the balanced humanities and engineering  curriculum with academies provides the  Graduate with all that he needs for any  job in the Air Force a man who has  successfully put together a balance of  science and the humanities his 1964  graduate major Fred Gregory currently a  NASA astronaut this is the National Air  and Space Museum inside will see the  history of man and atmospheric flight  and Space Flight I'm a member of the  shuttle program and I certainly hope to  be a part of this museum soon and it all  began at the Air Force Academy  I think just about everything at the  academy aim you towards space when I  entered the Academy I had a I had upon  hope of becoming a member of the space  team but it was a it was a far-off dream  it's like dreaming to be the president  Italy it's a goal but you never expected  to achieve it however when I selected my  courses to take I tried to take courses  that would broaden me the Aeronautics  courses the astronomy courses the  mechanical engineering courses the  physics courses of course all were the  faces and background for the work that  I'm doing now the English courses the  technical writing courses the speaking  courses all contributed and to my  success and I suppose that everybody  success we have in the program with the  three other Academy grad so I think we  were well represented for a total of 55  or 60 astronauts four Academy graduates  is a pretty good average 25 years ago  90% of the entering cadets had to  qualify for pilot training today that  requirement has been reduced to 60% one  of those who entered the Academy knowing  he would not be a pilot his 1976  graduate lieutenant Mickey Duvall five  minutes the panels that go on at that  time and he got to be information after  that  not every Academy graduate becomes an  air crew member many of us join the  support field for example myself I  joined and become a member of the  aircraft maintenance team as a cadet I  was able to participate in many of the  airmanship programs some of which were  the orientation flights also a third  lieutenant program and finally I'm most  proud of my parachuting that I  accomplished at the academy over the  years the Academy's airmanship program  has used many ways to open the door to  the world of flying in the stale plane  the student experiences the basic  principles of aerodynamics 50:43 exposes  the cadet to the sophistication modern  navigation equipment and techniques  those who are qualified for pilot  training taste the power flight in the  t41 lieutenant the raw sums up the  experience  I think that the programs are they  participating as a cadet has helped me  immensely and understanding what the  aircrew members are going through on the  flight line it's helped me to appreciate  their role as a air crew member got them  gone backlund class of 71 received the  Air Force Cross for his actions during  the mayagüez incident I've been very  fortunate my Air Force career to get an  opportunity to fly several different  types of aircraft  I began by flying rescue helicopters and  then transition into fixed-wing jets the  academy influenced me tremendously I  read something as the cadet in a book  that has that struck me as a cadet  that's a very young man and I remembered  ever since it's a quote by Winston  Churchill he and I'm paraphrasing  because I don't remember the word for  word but he said there comes a time in  every man's life and he is called upon  to do something very special something  for which he and he only has the  capabilities and the skills and instant  the necessary training  what a pity if the moment finds the man  unprepared and in my mind that's the  mission of the United States Air Force  Academy ensure that every cadet requires  the preparation develop the skills and  the training and the attitudes so that  when the moment finds him in his career  United States Air Force will be prepared  captain Don Backlund lost his life in an  aircraft accident in August 1979 well  this film was still in production
 

